WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.237 --> 00:00:04.014
Hi, I'm excited to share with
you this new course

2
00:00:04.014 --> 00:00:07.412
on using LangChain to
chat with your data. This

3
00:00:07.412 --> 00:00:10.433
is built in collaboration
with Harrison Chase, co-founder

4
00:00:10.433 --> 00:00:11.944
and CEO of LangChain.

5
00:00:13.097 --> 00:00:16.153
Large language models or
LLMs such as ChatGPT

6
00:00:16.153 --> 00:00:19.209
can answer questions about
a lot of topics,

7
00:00:19.209 --> 00:00:23.792
but an LLM in isolation knows
only what it was trained on,

8
00:00:23.792 --> 00:00:26.466
which doesn't
include your personal data, such

9
00:00:26.466 --> 00:00:31.050
as if you're in a company and have
proprietary documents not on

10
00:00:31.050 --> 00:00:35.633
the internet, as well as data or articles
that were written after

11
00:00:35.633 --> 00:00:37.161
the LLM was trained.

12
00:00:37.201 --> 00:00:42.615
So wouldn't it be useful if you or
others such as your customers can

13
00:00:42.615 --> 00:00:46.095
have a conversation with your own
documents and get

14
00:00:46.095 --> 00:00:48.415
questions answered using
information from those

15
00:00:48.415 --> 00:00:50.348
documents and using an LLM?

16
00:00:50.348 --> 00:00:54.061
In this short course,
we will cover how to use

17
00:00:54.061 --> 00:00:57.773
LangChain to chat with your data.
LangChain is an open-source

18
00:00:57.773 --> 00:01:00.371
developer framework for
building LLM applications. LangChain

19
00:01:00.371 --> 00:01:02.599
consists of several
modular components as

20
00:01:02.599 --> 00:01:05.568
well as more end-to-end templates. The
modular components

21
00:01:05.568 --> 00:01:08.167
in LangChain include
prompts, models, indexes, chains,

22
00:01:08.167 --> 00:01:11.508
and agents. For
a more detailed look at these

23
00:01:11.508 --> 00:01:14.849
components, you can see
our first course that I

24
00:01:14.849 --> 00:01:15.962
taught with Andrew.

25
00:01:16.021 --> 00:01:20.166
In this course, we will zoom in and
focus on one of the

26
00:01:20.166 --> 00:01:23.354
more popular use cases of LangChain,
how to use LangChain

27
00:01:23.354 --> 00:01:26.861
to chat with your data.
We will first cover how to

28
00:01:26.861 --> 00:01:29.731
use LangChain document
loaders to load data from a

29
00:01:29.731 --> 00:01:32.919
variety of exciting sources.
We will then touch on how

30
00:01:32.919 --> 00:01:34.832
to split these
documents into semantically

31
00:01:34.832 --> 00:01:37.702
meaningful chunks. This pre-processing
step may seem simple but

32
00:01:37.702 --> 00:01:39.296
has a lot of nuance.

33
00:01:39.354 --> 00:01:42.354
Next, we'll
give an overview of semantic search, a

34
00:01:42.354 --> 00:01:44.354
basic method for
fetching relevant information

35
00:01:44.354 --> 00:01:47.354
given a user question. This
is the easiest method

36
00:01:47.354 --> 00:01:50.021
to get started with
but there are several

37
00:01:50.021 --> 00:01:54.687
cases where it fails. We'll go
over these cases and then we'll go over

38
00:01:54.687 --> 00:01:59.354
how to fix them. We'll then show how
to use those retrieved documents to

39
00:01:59.354 --> 00:02:02.354
enable an LLM
to answer questions about a document

40
00:02:02.354 --> 00:02:06.021
but show that you're still missing
one key piece in order

41
00:02:06.021 --> 00:02:09.021
to fully recreate that chatbot experience.
Finally, we'll cover

42
00:02:09.021 --> 00:02:12.354
that missing piece, memory,
and show how to build a

43
00:02:12.354 --> 00:02:15.021
fully functioning chatbot through
which you can chat

44
00:02:15.021 --> 00:02:16.021
with your data.


45
00:02:16.079 --> 00:02:21.464
This will be an exciting short course.
We're grateful to Ankush Gola as well

46
00:02:21.464 --> 00:02:26.080
as Lance Martin from the LangChain
team for working on all the

47
00:02:26.080 --> 00:02:29.157
materials that you
hear Harrison present later, as

48
00:02:29.157 --> 00:02:32.234
well as on the deeplearning.ai side,
Geoff Ladwig

49
00:02:32.234 --> 00:02:33.388
and Diala Ezzeddine.

50
00:02:33.388 --> 00:02:37.249
In case you're going through this course
and decide you'd like

51
00:02:37.249 --> 00:02:41.461
a refresher on the basics of LangChain, I
encourage you to also

52
00:02:41.461 --> 00:02:44.620
take that earlier short course on
LangChain for LLM

53
00:02:44.620 --> 00:02:46.726
application development that
Harrison had mentioned

54
00:02:46.726 --> 00:02:50.587
as well. But with
that, let us now go on to

55
00:02:50.587 --> 00:02:53.394
the next video where
Harrison will show you

56
00:02:53.394 --> 00:02:56.553
how to use LangChain's very convenient
collection of document

57
00:02:56.553 --> 00:02:56.904
loaders.
